{
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Resource allocation as a linear programme\n",
        "\n",
        "Copyright 2026 Dr. Pavanam Thomas\n",
        "\n",
        "This notebook follows the laboratory sequence: **Problem → formalization → assumptions → computation/estimation → validation → interpretation → limitations**.\n",
        "\n",
        "The numbers below are illustrative coefficients, not a plant dataset. Run the cells to obtain the solution. Do not treat printed values in an old output as a substitute for re-running the code.\n",
        "\n",
        "Related formal work: [lean4-optimization-economics](https://github.com/pavanamthomas/lean4-optimization-economics). Related valuation identities that can be used as *inputs* to an objective: [quantitative-finance-models](https://github.com/pavanamthomas/quantitative-finance-models)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 1. Problem\n",
        "\n",
        "A planner chooses non-negative activity levels for two divisible processes. Each process uses three resources and contributes a constant amount to a linear objective. The question is prescriptive under the model: which mix maximises the stated objective given the stated resource limits? It is not a description of what a firm historically produced."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 2. Formalization\n",
        "\n",
        "Decision variables: \(x=(x_1,x_2)\\ge 0\).\n",
        "\n",
        "Parameters: contribution vector \(c\), technology matrix \(A\), endowment \(b\).\n",
        "\n",
        "Programme:\n",
        "\n",
        "\\[\n",
        "\\max_x\\, c^\\top x \\quad\\text{subject to}\\quad Ax\\le b,\\quad x\\ge 0.\n",
        "\\]\n",
        "\n",
        "Dual:\n",
        "\n",
        "\\[\n",
        "\\min_y\\, b^\\top y \\quad\\text{subject to}\\quad A^\\top y\\ge c,\\quad y\\ge 0.\n",
        "\\]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "from optmodels.checks import lp_is_feasible, lp_objective, lp_slacks\n",
        "from optmodels.linear_program import solve_lp_dual, solve_resource_allocation, textbook_resource_data\n",
        "from optmodels.plots import plot_lp_feasible_region\n",
        "\n",
        "c, A, b = textbook_resource_data()\n",
        "c, A, b"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 3. Assumptions\n",
        "\n",
        "- Linear technology and constant returns: resource use and contribution scale proportionally with \(x\).\n",
        "- Divisibility: fractional activity is allowed. If crews or batches are integer, this LP is a relaxation, not the operational plan.\n",
        "- Coefficients are known and constant. There is no estimation step in this notebook.\n",
        "- All relevant constraints are written in \(Ax\\le b\). Unwritten ramping, setups, or quality limits are outside the model."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 4. Computation\n",
        "\n",
        "The primal is solved by `scipy.optimize.linprog` (HiGHS) applied to minimise \(-c^\\top x\). The dual is solved as a separate LP so that strong duality can be checked without trusting a solver's marginal-value field."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "primal = solve_resource_allocation(c, A, b)\n",
        "dual = solve_lp_dual(c, A, b)\n",
        "primal.x, primal.objective, dual.x, dual.objective, primal.success, dual.success"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 5. Validation\n",
        "\n",
        "`optmodels.checks` substitutes the reported \(x\) into \(c^\\top x\) and \(b-Ax\). It does not call the solver. Complementary slackness is a property of this pair of optima, not a general licence to treat every dual variable as a market price."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "feasible = lp_is_feasible(primal.x, A, b)\n",
        "obj = lp_objective(primal.x, c)\n",
        "slacks = lp_slacks(primal.x, A, b)\n",
        "duality_gap = abs(obj - dual.objective)\n",
        "complementary = slacks * dual.x\n",
        "feasible, obj, slacks, duality_gap, complementary"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "fig = plot_lp_feasible_region(c, A, b, primal.x)\n",
        "fig"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 6. Interpretation\n",
        "\n",
        "The computed \(x^\\star\) is a recommended activity mix *if* the linear programme is accepted. Dual variables are derivatives of the modelled optimal value with respect to a right-hand side, in a neighbourhood where the optimal basis does not change. A slack resource has a zero shadow price in this solution; that is complementary slackness, not a claim that the resource is worthless in the organisation.\n",
        "\n",
        "Optimization is prescriptive under the model. It is not a descriptive account of observed production."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 7. Limitations\n",
        "\n",
        "- Illustrative \(A\), \(b\), and \(c\) are not estimated.\n",
        "- Integer restrictions, uncertainty, and dynamics are other models in this repository; they are not hidden inside this LP.\n",
        "- Sensitivity of \(x^\\star\) to a coefficient is still sensitivity inside this programme. If a constraint was omitted, the derivative is a derivative of the wrong value function.\n",
        "\n",
        "The audit questions that apply to every model, including this one, are in `MODEL_AUDIT_CHECKLIST.md`."
      ]
    }
  ],
